{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018UVBfjpHW1Ywi3DYk9vohG","type":"tool_result","content":"956:=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/api-server-hub.md ===\n957-# Concept: The API server as hub — \"watching, not telling\"\n958-\n959-**Home:** Chapter 3 §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n960-**Chapter 3's second-most-important idea**, after the control loop.\n961-\n962-## The four facts that compose into a shape\n963-\n964-1. \"The API server is the front end for the Kubernetes control plane.\"\n965-   [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]\n966-2. etcd is \"the backing store for all cluster data.\" [same]\n967-3. \"Kubernetes has a hub-and-spoke API pattern. All API usage from nodes and the Pods they\n968-   run terminates at the API server, and none of the other control-plane components are\n969-   designed to expose remote services.\"\n970-   [source: k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK]\n971-4. A controller \"might carry the action out itself; more commonly, in Kubernetes, a\n972-   controller will send messages to the API server that have useful side effects.\"\n973-   [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23]\n974-\n975-Chapter 3's image for the result: **\"Not a chain of command. A chart table: one surface\n976-everyone works from, and no orders issued across it.\"**\n977-\n978-## ⚓ The reusable sentence (verbatim)\n979-\n980-> **The coordination mechanism in Kubernetes is watching, not telling.** Components don't\n981-> send each other instructions. They observe shared state and act on what they see.\n982-\n983-## Two precisions the chapter is careful about — preserve both\n984-\n985-**1. etcd confinement is a RECOMMENDATION, not an invariant.** \"Access to etcd is\n986-equivalent to root permission in the cluster, so **ideally** only the API server should\n987-have access to it\" [source: k8s-docs-etcd-access-control-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK].\n988-Chapter 3 states this explicitly: \"Read that as what it is: a strong recommendation with a\n989-security reason behind it, not a law of physics.\" **Do not harden it downstream.**\n990-\n991-**2. The hub describes STATE movement, not every connection.** \"The API server does open\n992-connections to kubelets: that is how fetching Pod logs, attaching to a running container,\n993-and port-forwarding work. Those paths carry a session, not an instruction. Nothing\n994-travelling on them tells a kubelet what ought to be running.\"\n995-\n996-An earlier draft asserted the absolute form; a prior AUTHOR-REVIEW records that it was\n997-softened deliberately after the source was fetched. **Do not restore the absolute\n998-phrasing.**\n999-\n1000-## The submission story (component altitude)\n1001-\n1002-1. Request arrives at the API server; validated and persisted. **Nothing is running yet.**\n1003-2. The description is now part of cluster state, visible to anything watching.\n1004-3. The scheduler notices a Pod with no node, selects one, records the selection back\n1005-   through the API server.\n1006-4. The kubelet on that node notices and starts the containers through the runtime.\n1007-5. Status flows back the same way.\n1008-\n1009-\"Read the verbs. *Notices. Selects. Records. Notices.* At no point does one component\n1010-instruct another.\"\n1011-"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7eee19e-037a-467d-aefb-84c30699c926","uuid":"4490ff28-e03f-4f28-a92c-b6221737b5ce","timestamp":"2026-08-25T00:25:40.841Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"956:=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/api-server-hub.md ===\n957-# Concept: The API server as hub — \"watching, not telling\"\n958-\n959-**Home:** Chapter 3 §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n960-**Chapter 3's second-most-important idea**, after the control loop.\n961-\n962-## The four facts that compose into a shape\n963-\n964-1. \"The API server is the front end for the Kubernetes control plane.\"\n965-   [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]\n966-2. etcd is \"the backing store for all cluster data.\" [same]\n967-3. \"Kubernetes has a hub-and-spoke API pattern. All API usage from nodes and the Pods they\n968-   run terminates at the API server, and none of the other control-plane components are\n969-   designed to expose remote services.\"\n970-   [source: k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK]\n971-4. A controller \"might carry the action out itself; more commonly, in Kubernetes, a\n972-   controller will send messages to the API server that have useful side effects.\"\n973-   [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23]\n974-\n975-Chapter 3's image for the result: **\"Not a chain of command. A chart table: one surface\n976-everyone works from, and no orders issued across it.\"**\n977-\n978-## ⚓ The reusable sentence (verbatim)\n979-\n980-> **The coordination mechanism in Kubernetes is watching, not telling.** Components don't\n981-> send each other instructions. They observe shared state and act on what they see.\n982-\n983-## Two precisions the chapter is careful about — preserve both\n984-\n985-**1. etcd confinement is a RECOMMENDATION, not an invariant.** \"Access to etcd is\n986-equivalent to root permission in the cluster, so **ideally** only the API server should\n987-have access to it\" [source: k8s-docs-etcd-access-control-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK].\n988-Chapter 3 states this explicitly: \"Read that as what it is: a strong recommendation with a\n989-security reason behind it, not a law of physics.\" **Do not harden it downstream.**\n990-\n991-**2. The hub describes STATE movement, not every connection.** \"The API server does open\n992-connections to kubelets: that is how fetching Pod logs, attaching to a running container,\n993-and port-forwarding work. Those paths carry a session, not an instruction. Nothing\n994-travelling on them tells a kubelet what ought to be running.\"\n995-\n996-An earlier draft asserted the absolute form; a prior AUTHOR-REVIEW records that it was\n997-softened deliberately after the source was fetched. **Do not restore the absolute\n998-phrasing.**\n999-\n1000-## The submission story (component altitude)\n1001-\n1002-1. Request arrives at the API server; validated and persisted. **Nothing is running yet.**\n1003-2. The description is now part of cluster state, visible to anything watching.\n1004-3. The scheduler notices a Pod with no node, selects one, records the selection back\n1005-   through the API server.\n1006-4. The kubelet on that node notices and starts the containers through the runtime.\n1007-5. Status flows back the same way.\n1008-\n1009-\"Read the verbs. *Notices. Selects. Records. Notices.* At no point does one component\n1010-instruct another.\"\n1011-","numLines":56,"totalLines":56}}
