{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0144QXCtSWpAbE4GXHegCgVi","type":"tool_result","content":"933\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/namespace.md ===\n934\t# Concept: Namespaces — a scope for names\n935\t\n936\t**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n937\t\n938\t## Definition (verbatim)\n939\t\n940\t> In Kubernetes, **namespaces** provide a mechanism for isolating groups of resources\n941\t> within a single cluster. **Names of resources need to be unique within a namespace, but\n942\t> not across namespaces.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n943\t\n944\tChapter 4's compression: **a namespace is a scope for names.** \"Two ships on two different\n945\tregistries can carry the same name, and neither registry has to care.\"\n946\t\n947\t## Two structural constraints, both testable\n948\t\n949\t**Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another**, and **each Kubernetes resource can only\n950\tbe in one namespace.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n951\t\n952\t> 🪝 **Snag:** the instinct to build a hierarchy comes from cloud IAM (management groups →\n953\t> subscriptions → resource groups) and from Linux control-group trees. The platform will\n954\t> not accommodate it. **There is exactly one level.**\n955\t\n956\t## The restraint the documentation actually states\n957\t\n958\tPreparation material tends to present namespaces as a maturity signal. The documentation\n959\tdoes not: they are \"intended for use in environments with many users spread across\n960\tmultiple teams, or projects. For clusters with a few to tens of users, **you should not\n961\tneed to create or think about namespaces at all.** Start using them when you need the\n962\tfeatures they provide.\" For production, the guidance is to **not** use `default` — \"make\n963\tother namespaces and use those.\"\n964\t\n965\t## ⚠ The correction most guides skip\n966\t\n967\t> It is **not** necessary to use multiple namespaces to separate slightly different\n968\t> resources, such as different versions of the same software. **Use labels to distinguish\n969\t> resources within the same namespace.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n970\t\n971\tChapter 4 §5 supplies the *why*, and it is the sharpest sentence in the chapter on this\n972\ttopic: **\"Namespaces partition *names*. Labels partition *sets*.\"** Nearly everything in\n973\tKubernetes operates over sets — every controller, every Service, every policy — \"so\n974\tpartitioning by namespace when you meant to partition by set leaves the mechanism that\n975\twould have grouped your objects unable to see across the line you drew.\"\n976\t\n977\tThis is a documented instruction, not a stylistic preference. Preserve it as such.\n978\t\n979\t## The four initial namespaces\n980\t\n981\t| Namespace | What it is for |\n982\t|---|---|\n983\t| `default` | so you can start using a new cluster without first creating a namespace |\n984\t| `kube-system` | the namespace for objects created by the Kubernetes system |\n985\t| `kube-public` | readable by all clients including unauthenticated ones; mostly reserved for cluster usage |\n986\t| `kube-node-lease` | holds the Lease objects associated with each node, which let the kubelet send heartbeats so the control plane can detect node failure |\n987\t\n988\t**Two details examiners like.**\n989\t\n990\t1. **`kube-public` is a convention, not an enforcement.** \"The public aspect of this\n991\t   namespace is only a convention, not a requirement.\" Preparation material routinely\n992\t   states it as a hard property. **Do not harden this downstream** — same class of\n993\t   precision as Chapter 3's *\"ideally* only the API server should have access to etcd.\"\n994\t2. **`kube-node-lease` is the one people forget**, and it connects forward to node\n995\t   heartbeats and failure detection.\n996\t\n997\t## ⚑ RULE 6 — `Lease` ownership, settled\n998\t\n999\tChapter 3's ledger recorded `Lease → Ch 5 / Ch 12`. Chapter 4 bears it to Chapter 8.\n1000\t**B2 settles it in Chapter 8's favor**, verified on disk 2026-08-24:\n1001\t\n1002\t> `chapter-lineup.md` row 8 — *\"**D1.2** — kubectl syntax and verbs, kubeconfig… node\n1003\t> lifecycle (cordon/drain/uncordon, node conditions, **leases**), semantic versioning…\"*\n1004\t\n1005\tChapter 3's ledger row is stale and is corrected in `glossary.md`. **Chapter 5's and\n1006\tChapter 12's Stage 14 should not re-litigate this.**\n1007\t\n1008\t## Deliberately shallow here\n1009\t\n1010\tThe Service DNS form `<service-name>.<namespace-name>.svc.cluster.local` gets exactly one\n1011\tsentence's worth of treatment — enough to explain why a bare `<service-name>` resolves\n1012\tlocally and crossing namespaces needs the FQDN. What serves those records and how\n1013\tresolution proceeds is **Chapter 9's**. ResourceQuota is named and handed to **Chapter 8**.\n1014\tBoth deferrals are beared correctly.\n1015\t\n1016\t## Related\n1017\t\n1018\t[[cluster-scoped-resource]] · [[label-selector]] · [[configmap]] · [[kubernetes-object]]\n1019\t=== END WRITE ===\n1020\t\n1021\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/cluster-scoped-resource.md ===\n1022\t# Concept: Namespaced vs cluster-scoped\n1023\t\n1024\t**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n1025\t**B3 classification:** one of nine cross-cutting themes — **originates here.**\n1026\t\n1027\t## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — this is the canonical retrieval string)\n1028\t\n1029\t> **Not everything lives in a namespace.** Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses are\n1030\t> cluster-scoped, and so are namespace objects themselves. Namespace scoping applies only\n1031\t> to namespaced objects. **Any question about who may act on a resource has to start by\n1032\t> asking which side of this boundary the resource is on.**\n1033\t> [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n1034\t\n1035\tDocumentation wording: \"Namespace-based scoping is applicable **only for namespaced\n1036\tobjects** (Deployments, Services, and so on) and **not for cluster-wide objects**, such as\n1037\tStorageClasses, Nodes, and PersistentVolumes… namespace resources are not themselves in a\n1038\tnamespace, and low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any\n1039\tnamespace.\"\n1040\t\n1041\t## ⚑ USE ONE PHRASE. Chapter 4 did not coin a name.\n1042\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7eee19e-037a-467d-aefb-84c30699c926","uuid":"24d4fc00-c0ad-497a-a3f9-44e7184604bf","timestamp":"2026-08-25T00:26:00.774Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-04\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/namespace.md ===\n# Concept: Namespaces — a scope for names\n\n**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> In Kubernetes, **namespaces** provide a mechanism for isolating groups of resources\n> within a single cluster. **Names of resources need to be unique within a namespace, but\n> not across namespaces.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n\nChapter 4's compression: **a namespace is a scope for names.** \"Two ships on two different\nregistries can carry the same name, and neither registry has to care.\"\n\n## Two structural constraints, both testable\n\n**Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another**, and **each Kubernetes resource can only\nbe in one namespace.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n\n> 🪝 **Snag:** the instinct to build a hierarchy comes from cloud IAM (management groups →\n> subscriptions → resource groups) and from Linux control-group trees. The platform will\n> not accommodate it. **There is exactly one level.**\n\n## The restraint the documentation actually states\n\nPreparation material tends to present namespaces as a maturity signal. The documentation\ndoes not: they are \"intended for use in environments with many users spread across\nmultiple teams, or projects. For clusters with a few to tens of users, **you should not\nneed to create or think about namespaces at all.** Start using them when you need the\nfeatures they provide.\" For production, the guidance is to **not** use `default` — \"make\nother namespaces and use those.\"\n\n## ⚠ The correction most guides skip\n\n> It is **not** necessary to use multiple namespaces to separate slightly different\n> resources, such as different versions of the same software. **Use labels to distinguish\n> resources within the same namespace.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n\nChapter 4 §5 supplies the *why*, and it is the sharpest sentence in the chapter on this\ntopic: **\"Namespaces partition *names*. Labels partition *sets*.\"** Nearly everything in\nKubernetes operates over sets — every controller, every Service, every policy — \"so\npartitioning by namespace when you meant to partition by set leaves the mechanism that\nwould have grouped your objects unable to see across the line you drew.\"\n\nThis is a documented instruction, not a stylistic preference. Preserve it as such.\n\n## The four initial namespaces\n\n| Namespace | What it is for |\n|---|---|\n| `default` | so you can start using a new cluster without first creating a namespace |\n| `kube-system` | the namespace for objects created by the Kubernetes system |\n| `kube-public` | readable by all clients including unauthenticated ones; mostly reserved for cluster usage |\n| `kube-node-lease` | holds the Lease objects associated with each node, which let the kubelet send heartbeats so the control plane can detect node failure |\n\n**Two details examiners like.**\n\n1. **`kube-public` is a convention, not an enforcement.** \"The public aspect of this\n   namespace is only a convention, not a requirement.\" Preparation material routinely\n   states it as a hard property. **Do not harden this downstream** — same class of\n   precision as Chapter 3's *\"ideally* only the API server should have access to etcd.\"\n2. **`kube-node-lease` is the one people forget**, and it connects forward to node\n   heartbeats and failure detection.\n\n## ⚑ RULE 6 — `Lease` ownership, settled\n\nChapter 3's ledger recorded `Lease → Ch 5 / Ch 12`. Chapter 4 bears it to Chapter 8.\n**B2 settles it in Chapter 8's favor**, verified on disk 2026-08-24:\n\n> `chapter-lineup.md` row 8 — *\"**D1.2** — kubectl syntax and verbs, kubeconfig… node\n> lifecycle (cordon/drain/uncordon, node conditions, **leases**), semantic versioning…\"*\n\nChapter 3's ledger row is stale and is corrected in `glossary.md`. **Chapter 5's and\nChapter 12's Stage 14 should not re-litigate this.**\n\n## Deliberately shallow here\n\nThe Service DNS form `<service-name>.<namespace-name>.svc.cluster.local` gets exactly one\nsentence's worth of treatment — enough to explain why a bare `<service-name>` resolves\nlocally and crossing namespaces needs the FQDN. What serves those records and how\nresolution proceeds is **Chapter 9's**. ResourceQuota is named and handed to **Chapter 8**.\nBoth deferrals are beared correctly.\n\n## Related\n\n[[cluster-scoped-resource]] · [[label-selector]] · [[configmap]] · [[kubernetes-object]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/cluster-scoped-resource.md ===\n# Concept: Namespaced vs cluster-scoped\n\n**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**B3 classification:** one of nine cross-cutting themes — **originates here.**\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — this is the canonical retrieval string)\n\n> **Not everything lives in a namespace.** Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses are\n> cluster-scoped, and so are namespace objects themselves. Namespace scoping applies only\n> to namespaced objects. **Any question about who may act on a resource has to start by\n> asking which side of this boundary the resource is on.**\n> [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n\nDocumentation wording: \"Namespace-based scoping is applicable **only for namespaced\nobjects** (Deployments, Services, and so on) and **not for cluster-wide objects**, such as\nStorageClasses, Nodes, and PersistentVolumes… namespace resources are not themselves in a\nnamespace, and low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any\nnamespace.\"\n\n## ⚑ USE ONE PHRASE. Chapter 4 did not coin a name.\n","numLines":110,"startLine":933,"totalLines":1658}}}
